alth goes to 0 at death and other things too.\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach that's very controllable: **get hit exactly once on a screen with ONE enemy**. Are there screens with a single enemy? Hmm.\n\nActually, the best controlled damage source in Zelda near the start: **the Octoroks shoot rocks in a straight line; the rocks move.** \n\nYou know what? Let me just be systematic and accept noise, using iterative narrowing. But each round requires a hit and I keep dying — that's fine, dying just costs a continue.\n\nRound plan for health:\n1. Continue → full health (3 hearts).\n2. Stay on the start screen (no enemies) → snapshot hA.\n3. Walk west; take exactly one hit; IMMEDIATELY walk back east to the start screen (no enemies); snapshot hB.\n4. diff hA vs hB decreased → survivors.\nHmm, still lots of changes (screen, position).\n\nAlternative: do it all on the enemy screen:\n1. On enemy screen, full health, snapshot hA.\n2. Wait until hit (check by screenshot), snapshot hB.\n3. diff decreased, name d1.\n4. Wait until another hit, snapshot hC, diff from d1.\n5. Repeat until few.\n\nEach wait ~120 frames. The noise from moving enemies will be eliminated because their positions are pseudo-random and won't consistently decrease.\n\nActually here's a cleaner trick: **the health byte decreases by exactly 1 each hit** (half-heart units). So across 3 hits, the survivor moves e.g. 6→5→4→3 while timers decrease by more/less. I can use the \"step pattern\" judgement.\n\nAlso, I can use `watch` in play_input to trace candidate addresses! After round 1, I get survivors; I can watch them across hits and see which steps by exactly 1.\n\nGood plan. Let me execute:\n\nStep 1: Continue now (press Start at death menu).\nStep 2: Screenshot to confirm full hearts.\nStep 3: Go west (left+up 280).\nStep 4: snapshot hA.\nStep 5: wait 120 frames, screenshot, snapshot hB.\nStep 6: diff decreased → d1.\nStep 7: wait 120, snapshot hC, diff from d1 → d2. etc.\n\nActually, one concern: w