hearts.** If I write a value to SRAM and then go to file select, I can see the change. That gives me the SRAM layout, not RAM.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a neat trick: after selecting a file, the values are copied into RAM. If I modify the SRAM byte, then load the file, then the RAM counter gets a distinctive value... then I can find WHERE in RAM by searching for that distinctive value! \n\nThat's brilliant and cheap:\n1. At the title, use write_memory to SRAM ($6000-$67FF) — set a distinctive pattern.\n2. Load the file.\n3. search_memory in cpuDebug for the pattern → find the RAM addresses.\n\nBut I need to know the SRAM layout. Which I'd have to find. Hmm, circular again, but the SRAM is small (8KB) and I can write a distinctive signature to the whole SRAM and see what happens? That would corrupt the save.\n\nAlternatively, the reverse: find the RAM counters, then trace_writes to find where they're written from (the SRAM offset).\n\nOK, let me get back to basics and just do a cleaner differential for the *rupee* counter, which is a single clean event: gain a rupee.\n\nBut gaining a rupee requires surviving Octoroks. Let me use this tactic: go to the Octorok screen, kill Octoroks with sword beams from full health. Actually, at full health every sword swing shoots a beam. But I need to face them.\n\nHmm, alternatively — maybe there IS a bush on the start screen? No.\n\nHmm, wait! Actually, I just realized: the screen west of the start is where I was. It has bushes. If I cut a bush, maybe a rupee appears. But Octoroks interfere.\n\nAlternatively, go to a screen with bushes AND no enemies. Maybe north of the start screen? Let me explore a bit. Actually, the Octorok screen — there might be a screen full of bushes with no enemies.\n\nHmm, but exploration costs time.\n\nLet me reconsider: I'm at the start screen, paused, with full health. Let me first read $0660-$06FF to look for the counters.\n\nLet me also reconsider the \"write and observe\" feasibility: if the HUD does NOT redraw on a raw RAM write, I need an event to redraw. But the task's proof method is precisely \"write_memory and a screenshot in which the HUD shows the value\". So presumably in Zelda the HUD does redraw (maybe not every frame, but on some event) — or writing the count followed by a screen transition/HUD update shows it.\n\nActually, in Zelda the HUD counters (rupees/keys/bombs) are redrawn whenever they change (the game tracks them and updates the display). If I write to the RAM counter directly, the game's next HUD update... hmm, the game compares? Typically the game updates the HUD when the value changes via its own code, not by polling. So a raw write may not show until something triggers a full HUD redraw (like entering a cave or a screen transition).\n\nActually, in Zelda, when you pick up a rupee, the rupee display updates immediately. That update is triggered by the pickup code, which calls the HUD routine. A raw RAM write wouldn't trigger it.\n\nBUT: the hearts display in Zelda is updated every frame from the health value? Hmm.\n\nLet me just test empirically whether the HUD redraws: I'll write a bogus value to a candidate and step frames; if the HUD doesn't change, I'll force a screen transition (walk to another screen) and check.\n\nOK, let me first read more RAM.\n\nActually, let me try a totally different and MUCH more effective idea for finding the health byte, using `watch`:\n\nI know Link's health decreases on hits. Earlier, my d1 diff (n0→n1, 120 frames of damage) had 108 survivors. The health byte is among them. But I couldn't see all 64. Let me re-run that diff with a filter to reduce, or use `from` to intersect with another damage round.\n\nLet me do another damage round and intersect.\n\nPlan: \n- resume, go west, take damage, snapshot n2.\n- diff n1 vs n2 decreased from d1 → d2.\n- The health byte survives (it decreases on each hit), timers may not.\n\nThat's a solid approach! The problem before was that after n1 Link died. Now he's 