{"type":"thinking","text":"$0700-$07FF is all zeros. So the state isn't there.\n\nSo the game state is in $0000-$06FF. The counters/hearts must be in $0600-$06FF (or $0500-$05FF).\n\nSince $0600-$061D are heavily used by per-frame animation, the counters might be at $0630-$06FF.\n\nHmm, in my $0600-$06FF read: $0630=4, $0633=1, $0636=1, $0637=1, $0638=10, $0639-$0650=36, $0651=34,$0652=255, $0671=34,$0672=255, $0678=8, rest 0.\n\nHmm, no obvious 3 (hearts).\n\nWait — maybe hearts are stored as *half hearts* = 6, or as a different encoding. Or maybe the read happened at a moment when... hmm, Link's health was full? Let me check: the last screenshot showed damage. Hmm.\n\nHold on — maybe the *displayed* hearts come from a byte in $0500-$05FF or in zero page.\n\nLet me reconsider: **The save data**. In Zelda, the save data block in RAM might be at $0600? No...\n\nHmm, let me look at this from the SRAM side. The save file for Zelda stores: name, hearts (max), rupees, keys, bombs, items, etc. Let me examine SRAM to find the structure, then relate to RAM.\n\nActually — here's a thought: the HUD counters in Zelda are known to be at $0672-$0675 in the RAM? Hmm. My read showed $0671=34, $0672=255, $0673-$0677=0, $0678=8.\n\nHmm, that doesn't look like 0/0/0 counters plus 3 hearts.\n\nWAIT. Maybe my read of $0600-$06FF happened on a *different screen/state* where Link was in a cave or transition? Let me take a fresh screenshot and read again to correlate.\n\nActually, let me take a completely different and much more reliable approach:\n\n**Use the file-select screen to find the counters.** Here's the plan:\n1. Go to the title/file-select (save & quit or reset).\n2. At file select, the game reads the save slots from SRAM into RAM. The three files' data will be in RAM (probably at a fixed buffer).\n3. The HUD isn't shown there, but... hmm.\n\nAlternatively:\n\n**Use SAVE at the death menu**, then examine SRAM. The save writes the current counters to SRAM. With all counters 0 and hearts 3, the SRAM will have a recognizable block. Then I can modify SRAM (e.g., set rupees to 99), reload, and see the HUD — that gives me the SRAM address, and then I can find the RAM address by tracing the copy routine (trace_writes on the SRAM read? no, trace reads of SRAM).\n\nHmm, complex.\n\n**SIMPLEST DECISIVE IDEA**: Use `trace_writes` in READ mode on a RAM range while the HUD is being drawn. The HUD draws at screen load. Actually, the HUD counters are drawn by a routine that reads the counter bytes. If I trace reads over $0600-$06FF during a screen scroll, the PCs that read will include the HUD routine. But separating them is hard.\n\n**ANOTHER IDEA — the rupee count is displayed.** When I get a rupee, the HUD updates immediately. That update routine reads the rupee byte. If I trace reads on $0600-$06FF for a few frames right when a rupee is collected, the rupee byte will be read by the HUD routine from a distinct PC. \n\nBut getting a rupee is the problem!\n\nHmm, OK. Let me revisit getting a rupee — maybe I'm overcomplicating. In Zelda, **Octoroks drop rupees/pickups**. Also, **the \"bush\" on the screen may hide a rupee**. \n\nActually — the simplest: **Kill an Octorok with a sword beam from full health** (safe), then walk to the drop. Earlier, drops did appear.\n\nAlternatively — wait!! **There's a much easier way to gain a rupee: the \"Money Making Game\" is far. But the \"10 rupees\" in the cave? Hmm.**\n\nHmm, hold on, actually — I recall that on the screen just west of the start, if you cut certain bushes you can find a \"heart container\"? No.\n\nOK, let me try a *completely different* approach to the whole problem that's guaranteed to work:\n\n**Approach: brute-force write-and-look.**\n\nI know the counters affect the HUD. If the HUD updates on a *screen load*, I can:\n1. Write a test value to a candidate address.\n2. Scroll to the next screen (screen load → HUD redraw).\n3. Check the HUD.\n\nThe candidate set: I need to find where. But I can be systematic: write a distinctive value to a *range* of addresses at once and see which HUD field changes? If I write, say, 5 to $0600-$06FF all at once, the HUD would show something (but the game would break).\n\nBetter: binary search! Write a test value to a half of the range, scroll, check (does any HUD counter change to the test value?). This is O(log n) with screenshots. But writing to arbitrary RAM breaks the game.\n\nHmm, but actually: writing to RAM and then scrolling may crash the game.\n\nHmm.\n\nOK, let me approach from knowledge: Let me think about what's actually in $0600-$06FF in Zelda. I have decent recall that:\n- $0600-$0604: written every frame with 0 (from the trace).\n- The trace showed writes to $0609, $060A, $060D, $0615, $061C, $061D, etc.\n\nHmm.\n\nActually, hold on! Let me reconsider the trace output. It listed writes to addresses 1536-1565, i.e., $0600-$061D only. And in my read, bytes $0600-$061F were all nonzero (7,37,67,247,...). So $0600-$061F is the \"player/animation\" area.\n\nAnd bytes $0620-$06FF were mostly zero with a few values. Those few values might be the counters! \n\n$0630 = 4. $0633 = 1. $0636 = 1. $0637 = 1. $0638 = 10. $0639-$0650 = 36. $0651=34, $0652=255. $0671=34, $0672=255. $0678=8.\n\nHmm, none equal 3.\n\nUnless the hearts value isn't 3. Hmm, wait — maybe when I did that read, Link's health wasn't full. Let me correlate: the read was right after the continue and before going west? No — I did the read AFTER going west and taking damage (the sequence: continue → full hearts → read $0600-$06FF? no).\n\nTimeline: \n- over6 (game over) → play6 (after continue, full hearts on start screen) → diff → 15 hits.\n- Then read $0600-$06FF (the 256-byte read) — that was AFTER the over6/play6? Let me check: I read $0600-$06FF after the diff, while still on the start screen with full health. Wait no — the read happened right after the \"play6\" diff, before going west. Actually looking back: I did the read of 0x600/256 right afte